lesson.body:
Duck typing is flexible, but that flexibility has a cost: nothing stops you from forgetting to implement a method a class was supposed to have, and you typically only find out when the program crashes at runtime, potentially far from where the mistake was made. Abstract classes exist to close that gap. An abstract class is a parent class that explicitly declares certain methods as required, without providing a full implementation for them, and Python will refuse to let you create an instance of any subclass that has not filled in every required method.

Python provides this through the built-in `abc` module: import `ABC` as the base your abstract class inherits from, and mark any required method with the `@abstractmethod` decorator placed directly above it.

```python
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class PaymentMethod(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def pay(self, amount):
        pass

class CreditCard(PaymentMethod):
    def pay(self, amount):
        return f"Charged ${amount} to credit card."

class PayPal(PaymentMethod):
    def pay(self, amount):
        return f"Paid ${amount} via PayPal."

class Bitcoin(PaymentMethod):
    pass

cc = CreditCard()
print(cc.pay(50))

pp = PayPal()
print(pp.pay(20))

bc = Bitcoin()
```

The first two calls print `Charged $50 to credit card.` and `Paid $20 via PayPal.` exactly as expected, since both CreditCard and PayPal implement `pay`. But the final line, `bc = Bitcoin()`, never succeeds. Bitcoin inherits from PaymentMethod without providing its own `pay` method, so Python raises `TypeError: Can't instantiate abstract class Bitcoin without an implementation for abstract method 'pay'` (the exact wording can vary slightly between Python versions, but a TypeError is always raised). This check happens the instant you try to create a Bitcoin object, not later when something eventually calls `pay` and finds it missing. Trying to create `PaymentMethod()` directly fails the exact same way, since ABC classes with unimplemented abstract methods can never be instantiated on their own, only concrete subclasses that finish the job can be.

This is a stricter, more formal cousin of the polymorphism and duck typing you just learned. Where duck typing trusts that an object has the right method and finds out the hard way if it does not, an abstract class states the requirement up front and enforces it before the object ever gets used. Note that an abstract class is not limited to bare, empty abstract methods either. It can mix in fully working concrete methods alongside its abstract ones, giving every subclass shared logic for free while still demanding that each one supply the pieces that truly need to vary.
